{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792309940,"nonce":"SZeGgizq/4Lutw5Sk3pDqblauAU=","title":"CodeEditor: scrolling within one window of rows should build nothing","message":"Follow-up to the issue on `For` re-placing unchanged children; reported by the RayDE session, same benchmark.\n\n**What remains**\n\nWith unchanged children no longer re-placed, the cost stays while rows enter the window: 8 ms per frame at 11 px steps and 13 ms at 52 px steps (in the workbench, the machine busy, so noisy). Each new row builds about 30 elements and restyles them.\n\n**What would fix it**\n\n- Compute the window as first and end row indices, so a step that stays inside one window triggers nothing, whatever `For` does.\n- Pool a fixed set of row elements whose content is computed from the first row plus the index, so crossing a line boundary changes a few bindings and creates no elements.","files":null}]}